Q: scrollIntoView to apply to IFRAME only A: <p><code>scrollIntoView</code> now has <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/scrollIntoView#container" rel="nofollow noreferrer">a <code>container</code> option</a>, supported since Chrome 140 in September 2025 according to MDN and <a href="https://caniuse.com/wf-scroll-into-view-container" rel="nofollow noreferrer">caniuse</a> (<a href="https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/9452" rel="nofollow noreferrer">see proposal</a>).</p>
<p>You can use <code>scrollIntoView({ container: &quot;nearest&quot; })</code> to scroll only the nearest scroll container.</p>
<p>(As of writing, it appears that Safari and Firefox do not support this feature. However, Safari doesn't scroll the outer page by default in the same way that Chrome and Firefox do.)</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>document.getElementById("scroll").addEventListener("click", () =&gt; {
  document.getElementById("target").scrollIntoView({
    behavior: "smooth",
  })
})

document.getElementById("scrollBlockNearest").addEventListener("click", () =&gt; {
  document.getElementById("target").scrollIntoView({
    behavior: "smooth",
    block: "nearest",
  })
})

document.getElementById("scrollContainerNearest").addEventListener("click", () =&gt; {
  document.getElementById("target").scrollIntoView({
    behavior: "smooth",
    container: "nearest",
  })
})</code></pre>
